1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,475
This is the game telling me that my computer is ready to build missions.

2
00:00:04,375 --> 00:00:06,955
Seventeen checks, and no problems.

3
00:00:08,755 --> 00:00:11,782
By the end of this lecture, you'll have asked it the same question,

4
00:00:12,232 --> 00:00:14,382
and you'll know how to read the answer.

5
00:00:15,282 --> 00:00:18,168
And if you've never seen a window like this one, good,

6
00:00:18,618 --> 00:00:20,448
because this lecture is for you.

7
00:00:21,648 --> 00:00:26,763
Everything on your computer is a file, and files live in folders.

8
00:00:27,663 --> 00:00:29,174
A file is one named thing:

9
00:00:29,624 --> 00:00:32,620
a picture, a page, a program.

10
00:00:33,520 --> 00:00:35,558
A folder is a box with a name,

11
00:00:36,008 --> 00:00:38,723
and boxes go inside other boxes.

12
00:00:40,523 --> 00:00:44,212
This window, File Explorer, is one way to look inside a box.

13
00:00:45,112 --> 00:00:47,038
The command prompt is another.

14
00:00:47,938 --> 00:00:50,677
Today you'll use both of them, on the same box.

15
00:00:51,877 --> 00:00:53,576
So first, where is the game?

16
00:00:54,476 --> 00:00:56,930
You didn't choose its folder, the store did,

17
00:00:57,380 --> 00:00:59,207
so ask the store.

18
00:01:00,107 --> 00:01:02,882
In Steam, go to the library, right-click the game,

19
00:01:03,332 --> 00:01:06,866
then choose manage, and browse local files.

20
00:01:07,766 --> 00:01:09,802
In the itch app, right-click the game,

21
00:01:10,252 --> 00:01:13,080
then manage, and open folder in Explorer.

22
00:01:14,880 --> 00:01:17,915
Either way, a window opens on the game's folder,

23
00:01:18,365 --> 00:01:22,985
and you know you're in the right place when you see these four names.

24
00:01:23,885 --> 00:01:25,895
This one is the game itself.

25
00:01:26,345 --> 00:01:28,579
Data is everything it shows and plays.

26
00:01:29,029 --> 00:01:32,367
And these two are helpers that it brings along.

27
00:01:33,267 --> 00:01:34,891
Leave everything else alone.

28
00:01:36,091 --> 00:01:40,903
Now go into data, and then into missions.

29
00:01:41,803 --> 00:01:44,162
This is where you'll work for the whole course.

30
00:01:45,062 --> 00:01:47,305
Each of these folders is one mission,

31
00:01:47,755 --> 00:01:50,073
and yours will be one more, right beside them.

32
00:01:51,873 --> 00:01:54,418
Now look up here, at the address bar.

33
00:01:55,318 --> 00:01:57,912
If I click in the empty part of it,

34
00:01:58,362 --> 00:02:00,409
the names turn into one line of text.

35
00:02:01,309 --> 00:02:03,169
That line is a path,

36
00:02:03,619 --> 00:02:06,962
which is the full address of this folder.

37
00:02:07,862 --> 00:02:11,724
It reads: drive C, then the folder Cosmos,

38
00:02:12,174 --> 00:02:17,888
with data inside it, and missions inside that.

39
00:02:18,788 --> 00:02:21,492
So the backslash just means inside.

40
00:02:22,392 --> 00:02:25,712
Write your own path down, because you'll want it again.

41
00:02:26,912 --> 00:02:29,594
There's one setting to change, and you only change it once.

42
00:02:30,494 --> 00:02:33,289
Every file's name ends in a dot and a few letters.

43
00:02:33,739 --> 00:02:37,307
That's the extension, and it says what kind of file this is.

44
00:02:38,207 --> 00:02:40,644
And Windows hides it from you.

45
00:02:42,444 --> 00:02:45,466
So this file says sbs,

46
00:02:45,916 --> 00:02:48,395
when its real name is sbs.bat.

47
00:02:49,295 --> 00:02:52,432
Here's why that matters to you.

48
00:02:53,332 --> 00:02:58,129
In a few lectures, you'll save a file called mission.amd.

49
00:02:59,029 --> 00:03:03,063
If Windows quietly saves it as mission.amd, dot T X T,

50
00:03:03,513 --> 00:03:08,026
it still looks right on the screen, and the game can't find it.

51
00:03:09,826 --> 00:03:14,490
So open view, then show, and tick file name extensions.

52
00:03:15,390 --> 00:03:16,872
And now nothing is hidden.

53
00:03:18,072 --> 00:03:21,036
Now for the other window.

54
00:03:21,936 --> 00:03:23,930
It's the same click on the address bar,

55
00:03:24,380 --> 00:03:27,710
and this time I type C M D, and press Enter.

56
00:03:29,510 --> 00:03:30,778
This is the command prompt.

57
00:03:31,678 --> 00:03:33,058
Look at its last line:

58
00:03:33,508 --> 00:03:35,944
it's the path we just read, and then an arrow.

59
00:03:36,844 --> 00:03:38,883
The path is where this window is standing,

60
00:03:39,333 --> 00:03:41,599
and the arrow means it's my turn.

61
00:03:43,399 --> 00:03:47,199
One warning, though: Windows has a second program that looks just like this one,

62
00:03:47,649 --> 00:03:49,903
and it's called PowerShell.

63
00:03:50,803 --> 00:03:54,091
You can tell it by the letters P S at the start of the line,

64
00:03:54,541 --> 00:03:56,938
and our tool won't run there.

65
00:03:57,838 --> 00:04:01,908
So if you ever see P S, type C M D, press Enter, and it goes away.

66
00:04:03,108 --> 00:04:06,076
Here's my first command: D I R, and Enter.

67
00:04:06,976 --> 00:04:09,222
It lists what's in this folder.

68
00:04:10,122 --> 00:04:14,433
Where a line says D I R in brackets, that's a folder.

69
00:04:14,883 --> 00:04:19,881
Where it has a number, that's a file, and the number is its size.

70
00:04:21,681 --> 00:04:24,723
And it's the same list that File Explorer shows,

71
00:04:25,173 --> 00:04:27,645
because these are two windows onto one folder.

72
00:04:28,545 --> 00:04:30,203
There are four keys to know.

73
00:04:31,103 --> 00:04:33,076
Enter runs the line.

74
00:04:33,526 --> 00:04:36,240
The up arrow brings back the last line I ran.

75
00:04:36,690 --> 00:04:38,035
Backspace rubs out a letter,

76
00:04:38,485 --> 00:04:40,544
and Escape clears the whole line.

77
00:04:41,744 --> 00:04:44,155
The game comes with a tool for mission writers.

78
00:04:45,055 --> 00:04:47,991
It's called sbs, and it lives in this folder:

79
00:04:48,441 --> 00:04:50,104
those were its two files.

80
00:04:51,004 --> 00:04:57,267
A command to it is the word sbs, a space, and then what I want.

81
00:04:57,717 --> 00:04:59,425
So I'll ask it for its version.

82
00:05:01,225 --> 00:05:02,470
Two lines come back:

83
00:05:02,920 --> 00:05:05,212
its version, and the folder it lives in,

84
00:05:05,662 --> 00:05:08,456
which should be the path you wrote down.

85
00:05:09,356 --> 00:05:11,503
Read the version as two whole numbers,

86
00:05:11,953 --> 00:05:17,373
so this is nought, thirteen, and thirteen is newer than nine.

87
00:05:18,273 --> 00:05:21,777
The course needs twelve or higher.

88
00:05:23,577 --> 00:05:26,294
The tool is mended and published again from time to time.

89
00:05:27,194 --> 00:05:32,010
To get the newest, type sbs update, and wait for it to finish.

90
00:05:32,910 --> 00:05:35,701
It says updated, and where.

91
00:05:36,151 --> 00:05:37,495
Then ask for the version again.

92
00:05:38,395 --> 00:05:42,013
Do that whenever a page of this course names a higher number than yours.

93
00:05:43,213 --> 00:05:44,277
Now the real question.

94
00:05:45,177 --> 00:05:50,252
It's sbs doctor, and then a switch: two hyphens, and E N V.

95
00:05:51,152 --> 00:05:53,553
A switch changes how a command works,

96
00:05:54,003 --> 00:05:56,810
and this one means, look at this computer only.

97
00:05:58,610 --> 00:06:03,993
It prints about thirty lines, and most of them are written for a programmer.

98
00:06:04,893 --> 00:06:06,442
You need three things from it.

99
00:06:07,342 --> 00:06:10,114
The first is the last line, which says no problems,

100
00:06:10,564 --> 00:06:13,324
and that's your answer.

101
00:06:14,224 --> 00:06:16,671
The second is the mark at the start of each row.

102
00:06:17,571 --> 00:06:19,975
O K means it's fine.

103
00:06:20,425 --> 00:06:26,015
Two hyphens means something extra isn't here, and that's not a fault.

104
00:06:26,915 --> 00:06:30,310
Two exclamation marks would be a problem, and you have none.

105
00:06:32,110 --> 00:06:36,475
And the third: a line that's set further in belongs to the row above it.

106
00:06:37,375 --> 00:06:41,210
Under two hyphens, it says what the extra thing is for.

107
00:06:41,660 --> 00:06:43,098
It's not an instruction.

108
00:06:43,998 --> 00:06:46,621
A few rows like that are normal on a writer's computer.

109
00:06:47,821 --> 00:06:51,349
Windows finds sbs in one folder only, and it's this one.

110
00:06:52,249 --> 00:06:54,587
Watch what happens somewhere else.

111
00:06:55,487 --> 00:06:59,242
C D means change folder, so I'll go into a mission.

112
00:07:00,142 --> 00:07:02,384
The line on the left has changed.

113
00:07:03,284 --> 00:07:04,931
Now I ask for the version,

114
00:07:05,381 --> 00:07:08,466
and it says, not recognized.

115
00:07:10,266 --> 00:07:14,147
Read that as: there's nothing called sbs where you're standing.

116
00:07:15,047 --> 00:07:18,549
And that's true, because it's one folder up.

117
00:07:19,449 --> 00:07:22,293
C D and two dots means go up one.

118
00:07:23,193 --> 00:07:26,206
Then the up arrow, twice, and Enter,

119
00:07:26,656 --> 00:07:29,370
and it works again.

120
00:07:31,170 --> 00:07:33,864
You'll see that message again.

121
00:07:34,764 --> 00:07:38,237
When you do, read the start of the line before anything else.

122
00:07:39,137 --> 00:07:42,039
And if all else fails, close the window,

123
00:07:42,489 --> 00:07:44,640
and open a new one from the address bar.

124
00:07:45,840 --> 00:07:48,392
Here are three slips, so that you've seen them once.

125
00:07:49,292 --> 00:07:51,537
The first is a misspelled command:

126
00:07:51,987 --> 00:07:54,952
the tool says no such command, and guesses what I meant.

127
00:07:55,852 --> 00:07:58,643
The second is a command copied from a word processor.

128
00:07:59,543 --> 00:08:03,341
It turned my two hyphens into one long dash,

129
00:08:03,791 --> 00:08:06,800
and the tool takes that dash for a folder name.

130
00:08:07,700 --> 00:08:10,270
So type the hyphens here, in this window.

131
00:08:12,070 --> 00:08:15,268
And the third you've just seen, which is the wrong folder.

132
00:08:16,168 --> 00:08:19,984
The page has tables of more than twenty slips, and each one was tried.

133
00:08:21,184 --> 00:08:24,296
Without the switch, doctor looks at every mission too.

134
00:08:25,196 --> 00:08:29,247
It scrolls, and that's fine, because I read the end first.

135
00:08:30,147 --> 00:08:32,065
It says no problems, so I'm done.

136
00:08:33,865 --> 00:08:35,593
Each mission has a part like this one:

137
00:08:36,043 --> 00:08:39,828
three rows, and all of them O K.

138
00:08:40,728 --> 00:08:43,751
If the end ever names a mission, I scroll up to that mission,

139
00:08:44,201 --> 00:08:45,808
and I keep two rules.

140
00:08:46,708 --> 00:08:49,008
The first: is it my mission?

141
00:08:49,458 --> 00:08:52,448
One that came with the game isn't mine, so I leave it.

142
00:08:53,348 --> 00:08:56,566
The second: the line under a problem suggests a command.

143
00:08:57,466 --> 00:09:00,669
Don't type it just because a report told you to.

144
00:09:02,469 --> 00:09:05,777
And never type sbs fetch on its own,

145
00:09:06,227 --> 00:09:09,364
because that replaces a whole mission folder.

146
00:09:10,264 --> 00:09:13,820
To look at one mission, I put its folder's name after the command.

147
00:09:15,020 --> 00:09:16,956
Now do it without me.

148
00:09:17,856 --> 00:09:19,939
Close both of the windows,

149
00:09:20,389 --> 00:09:24,029
then find the folder, open the prompt, and read the line.

150
00:09:24,929 --> 00:09:28,952
Run doctor on one mission, and make three slips on purpose.

151
00:09:29,852 --> 00:09:33,401
Then write down your path, and your version.

152
00:09:35,201 --> 00:09:37,084
Next time, you get an editor,

153
00:09:37,534 --> 00:09:40,961
and you make your first mission in the window you opened today.
